  "prompt": "Which methods did Socrates employ to challenge the prevailing thoughts of his time?",
  "answer": "Socrates challenged prevailing beliefs mainly through dialogue, encouraging people to examine whether their opinions could withstand careful questioning. His methods included:\n\n- **Socratic questioning:** He asked probing questions about familiar ideas such as justice, courage, and virtue, exposing assumptions people took for granted.\n- **Elenchus, or cross-examination:** He drew out the implications of someone’s claims, then revealed contradictions between them, showing where their understanding was incomplete.\n- **Professed ignorance:** Rather than presenting himself as an authority, he claimed not to know and invited others to explain. This approach is often called **Socratic irony**.\n- **Seeking definitions and testing examples:** When someone defined a virtue, he offered counterexamples to see whether the definition held in every case.\n- **Challenging authority and convention:** He questioned prominent Athenians and argued that tradition, reputation, and popular agreement did not by themselves establish truth.\n\nHis aim was to encourage intellectual humility and a more examined, virtuous life. We know his methods chiefly through accounts by writers such as Plato and Xenophon, since Socrates left no writings.",
